    "full_answer": "Authentication in requests works by using authentication handlers. These handlers are classes that implement the authentication logic for different types of authentication, such as basic authentication, digest authentication, and proxy authentication.\n\nThe `requests.auth` module contains the authentication handlers for requests. The handlers are implemented as classes that inherit from the `AuthBase` class.\n\nThe authentication process works as follows:\n\n1. The user creates an instance of the authentication handler class, passing in the necessary credentials.\n2. The user adds the authentication handler to the request object using the `auth` parameter.\n3. When the request is sent, the authentication handler is called to authenticate the request.\n4. The authentication handler returns a tuple containing the authentication data and the authentication method.\n\nThe authentication data is used to authenticate the request, and the authentication method is used to determine how the authentication data should be used.\n\nFor example, the `HTTPBasicAuth` handler takes a username and password as credentials, and returns a tuple containing the username and password, and the authentication method `basic`.\n\nThe `HTTPDigestAuth` handler takes a username and password as credentials, and returns a tuple containing the username and password, and the authentication method `digest`.\n\nThe `HTTPProxyAuth` handler takes a proxy URL and credentials as credentials, and returns a tuple containing the proxy URL and credentials, and the authentication method `proxy`.\n\nThe authentication handlers are implemented in the `requests.auth` module, and can be used by passing an instance of the handler class to the `auth` parameter of the request object.\n\nFor example:\n```python\nfrom requests.auth import HTTPBasicAuth\n\nauth = HTTPBasicAuth('username', 'password')\nresponse = requests.get('https://example.com', auth=auth)\n```\nThis code creates an instance of the `HTTPBasicAuth` handler, passing in the username and password as credentials. It then adds the authentication handler to the request object using the `auth` parameter, and sends the request.\n\nThe authentication handler is called to authenticate the request, and returns a tuple containing the authentication data and the authentication method. The authentication data is used to authenticate the request, and the authentication method is used to determine how the authentication data should be used.\n\nThis is a basic overview of how authentication works in requests. For more information, please refer to the requests documentation.",
